Original has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} (Köppen ''Am'') with consistently warm temperatures year-round and a pronounced wet season from May to November. The dense urban fabric generates a significant {{w|urban heat island}} effect, with temperatures in the city centre regularly exceeding those of the surrounding coastal areas by 2–3 °C, particularly at night and during the drier season.

== History ==

{{main|Sant Cristòfor#History}}

Original is the site of the original Volisanian settlement that gave Sant Cristòfor its start. The Birú settlement on the eastern shore of the bay was displaced in 1524, and the colonial town grew around the natural harbour. The original street grid of the old town — now the southern barris of [[El Raval]], [[La Tanneria]], and [[Sant Pere del Camí]] — still follows the irregular medieval pattern of narrow lanes and small plazas.

During the colonial period, the city expanded northward along the bay shore, with the commercial waterfront (now [[El Centre]] and the [[Districte Financer]]) developing as the principal port district. The 1866 racial districting ordinance formalised the city's ethnic geography: Black Balboans in the south, Fosian Balboans in the east, White Balboans in the north, and the mixed commercial class along the waterfront. This pattern remains visible in the ciutat's demographics today.

The construction of the [[Grand Train Terminal|Estació Gran]] (1868–1872) at [[Plaça Major]] anchored the northern expansion of the city. The Zong administration (1899–1950) rebuilt much of the eastern neighbourhoods in the characteristic Zong-era apartment block style, established the [[Fosian Balboan|Fosian]] cultural quarter around [[Turó del Temple]], and expanded the city's grid eastward into [[L'Eixample Est]].

Post-reunification, the bay shore was redeveloped with modern skyscrapers, the financial district expanded, and the former docklands of [[Molls Superiors]] and [[Molls Inferiors]] were converted to residential and commercial use. The [[Districte Càrnic]] and [[Districte Càrnic Sud]] remain working-class meatpacking districts, a rare surviving industrial zone in the increasingly gentrified ciutat.

